WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.640 --> 00:00:04.720
Spider-Man's Marvel Cinematic Universe&nbsp;
journey began way back in 2016&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:04.720 --> 00:00:07.760
as a part of the ensemble film&nbsp;
"Captain America: Civil War."&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:07.760 --> 00:00:11.280
In the years since he has found great&nbsp;
success and failure — certainly doing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:11.280 --> 00:00:15.280
more than previous live-action incarnations&nbsp;
of the web-slinger ever had a chance to.

00:00:15.280 --> 00:00:19.040
He's gone toe-to-toe with Earth-bound&nbsp;
supervillains, faced down the Mad Titan,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:19.040 --> 00:00:23.840
Thanos in a bid to save the universe, and&nbsp;
even came back from beyond the grave. However,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:23.840 --> 00:00:28.880
"Spider-Man: No Way Home" presented him with&nbsp;
arguably his biggest challenge yet in the MCU.

00:00:28.880 --> 00:00:32.720
Following a magical, multiverse-collapsing&nbsp;
mishap courtesy of Doctor Strange,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:32.720 --> 00:00:36.640
several iconic Spider-Man villains&nbsp;
poured into the main MCU timeline.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:36.640 --> 00:00:40.880
Standouts from Sam Raimi's "Spider-Man"&nbsp;
trilogy Green Goblin, Dr. Otto Octavius,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:40.880 --> 00:00:44.080
and Sandman returned to the&nbsp;
silver screen, but that's not all.

00:00:44.080 --> 00:00:47.520
Foes from Marc Webb's "Amazing&nbsp;
Spider-Man" films reemerged as well,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:47.520 --> 00:00:51.920
those being the Lizard and Electro. The latter&nbsp;
got quite the makeover this time around,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:51.920 --> 00:00:55.920
but simultaneously left fans scratching&nbsp;
their heads by the time the credits rolled.

00:00:55.920 --> 00:01:00.080
The MCU Spider-Man three-quel was a&nbsp;
mind-bending, continuity-straining event,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:00.080 --> 00:01:03.680
and while it was a ton of fun to reconnect&nbsp;
with all the old heroes and villains,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:03.680 --> 00:01:08.400
it did result in a few confusing moments.&nbsp;
Fans will likely be unpacking all the various&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:08.400 --> 00:01:13.040
plot conflicts created by "No Way Home" for a&nbsp;
generation, but there's one in particular that we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:13.040 --> 00:01:18.480
just can't shake. There's a big Electro-related&nbsp;
plot hole in "No Way Home." Here's what it is.

00:01:18.480 --> 00:01:22.800
As noted previously, the reason why so many&nbsp;
Spider-Man rogues from across time and space&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:22.800 --> 00:01:28.080
converged on the MCU Earth had to do with Doctor&nbsp;
Strange's botched spell. As he tried to magically&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:28.080 --> 00:01:32.240
force the world to forget that Peter Parker is&nbsp;
Spider-Man, the wall-crawler in question kept&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:32.240 --> 00:01:37.360
talking and breaking his focus. Seems like Tom&nbsp;
Holland has trouble keeping quiet in any reality.

00:01:37.360 --> 00:01:40.960
As a result, Strange's cast went&nbsp;
haywire, and before he could contain it,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:40.960 --> 00:01:45.440
a few sinister bad guys slipped through the&nbsp;
cracks. According to the former Sorcerer Supreme,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:45.440 --> 00:01:49.520
the flub brought over people who knew that Peter&nbsp;
Parker and Spider-Man were one and the same.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:49.520 --> 00:01:52.160
That goes a long way toward&nbsp;
explaining the rogues' gallery&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:52.160 --> 00:01:56.480
that our Spidey trifecta had to face off&nbsp;
against, but there is one outlier here.

00:01:56.480 --> 00:02:01.280
This concept makes sense for all of the villains&nbsp;
except Electro. Fans of the Garfield-starring "The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:01.280 --> 00:02:06.080
Amazing Spider-Man" flicks will likely recall that&nbsp;
Foxx's electrified baddy never actually learned&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:06.080 --> 00:02:10.720
his Spidey's secret identity during his sole&nbsp;
previous appearance in "The Amazing Spider-Man 2."

00:02:10.720 --> 00:02:14.560
Perhaps this little detail simply slipped&nbsp;
through the cracks — there is a lot of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:14.560 --> 00:02:19.120
continuity to wrangle, after all — but Electro's&nbsp;
ignorance of Spider-Man's identity actually made&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:19.120 --> 00:02:23.280
it into the script. The villain addresses it&nbsp;
explicitly in "No Way Home" when he mentions&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:23.280 --> 00:02:27.360
that he always thought Spider-Man was&nbsp;
Black. So, what's really going on here?

00:02:27.360 --> 00:02:31.360
The easy answer is that Marvel Studios and&nbsp;
Sony simply wanted to play to the fans and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:31.360 --> 00:02:36.080
give Jamie Foxx another chance to portray&nbsp;
Max Dillon. As for in-universe explanations,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:36.080 --> 00:02:39.680
fans have begun to pull some ideas&nbsp;
together to explain this plot hole away,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:39.680 --> 00:02:43.360
which is awfully generous of them.&nbsp;
Referring to a line by Electro in "No&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:43.360 --> 00:02:47.840
Way Home" concerning what he was doing before&nbsp;
he jumped to the MCU, on Redditor posted,

00:02:47.840 --> 00:02:49.680
"Assuming Strange was right about the spell,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:49.680 --> 00:02:53.200
perhaps Max absorbed some data that&nbsp;
gave him Spider-Man's identity."

00:02:53.200 --> 00:02:57.360
Another user also brings up an interesting&nbsp;
idea in the same Reddit thread, stating,

00:02:57.360 --> 00:03:01.520
"Most likely the villains of No Way Home were&nbsp;
pulled from universes that have history extremely&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:01.520 --> 00:03:06.080
similar to but not quite identical to the&nbsp;
events of the Raimi and Webb Spider-Man movies."

00:03:06.080 --> 00:03:10.080
Octavius' inability to recognize his&nbsp;
friend Curt Connors as the Lizard and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:10.080 --> 00:03:13.760
Sandman constantly maintaining his&nbsp;
sandy state — totally flying in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:13.760 --> 00:03:17.120
face of their Raimi incarnations&nbsp;
— lend credence to this theory.

00:03:17.120 --> 00:03:20.800
There's also the solution that a few people&nbsp;
brought up, claiming that perhaps Strange didn't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:20.800 --> 00:03:25.920
fully understand the spell or misinterpreted it&nbsp;
in some way. There's also the timeless answer&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:25.920 --> 00:03:30.960
that Electro slipped into the MCU because comics&nbsp;
are crazy and any attempt to explain away every&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:30.960 --> 00:03:35.600
little asynchronous detail is a fool's errand.&nbsp;
Regardless of the true answer, we can only hope&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:35.600 --> 00:03:39.600
that somehow a future MCU installment&nbsp;
will put this Electro plot hole to bed.

